@format 1
@id media-loading.common.file-drops
@title File drops
@section Media loading
@area Common
@entry viewer-drop, window-drop
@summary Trace file-system drops through the list-backed paths reachable in Cadroue, including both media/path eligibility and the WPF AllowedEffects gate.
@related media-loading.common.files-intake, media-loading.in-merge
@tag media
@tag loading
@tag common
@tag drag-drop

@alias viewer = Cadroue.UIShell.PPanels.PViewer
@alias window = Cadroue.UIShell.PMainWindow.PWindow
@alias workspace = Cadroue.UIShell.PControlBar.PWorkspace

[viewer-drop] Viewer overlay file drop <input>
~ This starts with viewer overlay file drop.
[Technical explanation]
- Receive a file-system drop on a media-tab viewer overlay; every currently constituted media-tab viewer attaches PDropPathsChange to its Files list, so the reachable application path is list-backed @ viewer.PDropHandle(...)
> viewer-path-check

[window-drop] Window-level file drop <input>
~ This starts with window-level file drop.
[Technical explanation]
- Receive the routed window drop; PDropHandle first checks whether the original source belongs to a Merge PGroup @ window.PDropHandle(...)
> window-owner-check

[viewer-path-check] Viewer dropped set has an eligible path <decision>
~ The program checks whether the dropped items include a supported media file or folder.
[Technical explanation]
- In the list-backed branch, PDropEffectRead requires at least one dropped directory or supported media path before consulting the drag operation allowed by WPF @ viewer.PDropEffectRead(...)
? "At least one eligible path" > viewer-effect-check
? "No eligible path" > viewer-no-path

[window-owner-check] Merge group owns routed drop <decision>
~ The program checks merge group owns routed drop.
[Technical explanation]
- PDropGroupCheck walks the original-source ancestry; when it reaches a PGroup, the window handler returns without handling the drop so the Merge group can own it @ window.PDropGroupCheck(...)
? "Inside Merge PGroup" > merge-group-owned
? "Not inside Merge PGroup" > window-list-check

[viewer-no-path] Viewer path eligibility rejected <output>
~ This path ends with viewer path eligibility rejected.
[Technical explanation]
= No Files intake starts because no dropped directory or supported media path exists.

[viewer-effect-check] Viewer drag operation allowed <decision>
~ The program checks viewer drag operation allowed.
[Technical explanation]
- PDropAllowedRead accepts Copy first, otherwise Move, otherwise Link when that effect is present in DragEventArgs.AllowedEffects; if none is allowed it returns None @ viewer.PDropAllowedRead(...)
? "Copy, Move, or Link allowed" > viewer-publish-paths
? "No supported effect allowed" > viewer-no-effect

[merge-group-owned] Merge group owns routed drop <output>
~ This path ends with merge group owns routed drop.
[Technical explanation]
= The window handler leaves the event to the Merge PGroup; the Merge scenario map covers that group-specific path.

[window-list-check] Reachable active Files list <decision>
~ The program checks reachable active Files list.
[Technical explanation]
- For the current workspace, the window uses pListActive when present; Worklist has neither Files nor a viewer, and every current workspace that has a viewer also has Files @ window.PDropEffectRead(...)
  @ workspace.PWorkspaceSurfaceCreate(...)
? "Active Files list exists" > window-path-check
? "No reachable Files drop path" > window-no-list

[viewer-publish-paths] Publish complete viewer drop set <process>
~ The dropped paths are sent to the active Files list.
[Technical explanation]
- PDropHandle marks the routed event handled and invokes PDropPathsChange with the complete dropped path set, not only the path that made the eligibility test succeed @ viewer.PDropHandle(...)
> map:media-loading.common.files-intake

[viewer-no-effect] Viewer drag effect rejected <output>
~ This path ends with viewer drag effect rejected.
[Technical explanation]
= No Files intake starts because DragEventArgs.AllowedEffects offered none of Copy, Move, or Link.

[window-path-check] Window dropped set has an eligible path <decision>
~ The program checks whether the dropped items include a supported media file or folder.
[Technical explanation]
- In the active-list branch, PDropEffectRead requires at least one dropped directory or supported media path before consulting AllowedEffects @ window.PDropEffectRead(...)
? "At least one eligible path" > window-effect-check
? "No eligible path" > window-no-path

[window-no-list] Window has no reachable list-backed drop path <output>
~ This path ends with window has no reachable list-backed drop path.
[Technical explanation]
= No media-loading path starts from the window handler.

[window-effect-check] Window drag operation allowed <decision>
~ The program checks window drag operation allowed.
[Technical explanation]
- PDropAllowedRead accepts Copy first, otherwise Move, otherwise Link when present in DragEventArgs.AllowedEffects; if none is allowed it returns None @ window.PDropAllowedRead(...)
? "Copy, Move, or Link allowed" > window-add-paths
? "No supported effect allowed" > window-no-effect

[window-no-path] Window path eligibility rejected <output>
~ This path ends with window path eligibility rejected.
[Technical explanation]
= No Files intake starts because no dropped directory or supported media path exists.

[window-add-paths] Add complete drop set to active Files <process>
~ This step handles add complete drop set to active Files.
[Technical explanation]
- PDropHandle marks the routed event handled and passes the complete dropped path set to the active Files list @ window.PDropHandle(...)
> map:media-loading.common.files-intake

[window-no-effect] Window drag effect rejected <output>
~ This path ends with window drag effect rejected.
[Technical explanation]
= No Files intake starts because DragEventArgs.AllowedEffects offered none of Copy, Move, or Link.
! Under the current workspace constitution, PViewer and PWindow contain fallback direct-viewer branches for a viewer with no Files path, but no currently constructed Cadroue workspace exposes that combination; those component-only branches are therefore not represented as reachable application flows.
